{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"torus network topology torus network topology","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md","fileName":"Torus Network Topology.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Torus Network Topology","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"torus network topology torus network topology","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md","fileName":"Torus Network Topology.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Torus Network Topology","heading":"Torus Network Topology","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A torus topology adds wraparound links to a ring or multidimensional mesh, so nodes at opposite edges become neighbors. The extra connections reduce maximum path length and remove the special boundary behavior of an open mesh.\n\nRouting must account for multiple directions and possible contention around each dimension. The regular structure suits grid-shaped parallel computations, but additional links increase implementation cost and do not eliminate congestion from an imbalanced communication pattern.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Distributed Memory and Message Passing","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Torus Network Topology.md::2","sourceKey